WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.000
It's a family outing in Lewis Bay Hyannisport for the Kennedys.

00:09.000 --> 00:13.600
The President, accompanied by his convalescing father, his brothers, and a full complement

00:13.600 --> 00:18.240
of children, relaxes from the cares of Washington with a day on the water.

00:18.240 --> 00:23.420
Mrs. Kennedy swims with astronaut John Glenn, a weekend guest, before they team up to put

00:23.420 --> 00:25.560
on an aqua show of their own.

00:25.560 --> 00:30.180
The President stays on the sidelines, as Mrs. Kennedy and high-flying Glenn practically

00:30.180 --> 00:32.840
go into orbit aboard water skis.

00:32.840 --> 00:37.240
Mrs. Ethel Kennedy, wife of the Attorney General, is at the controls of the towboat, and she

00:37.240 --> 00:38.980
turns on the steam.

00:38.980 --> 00:44.560
The astronaut and the First Lady put on a dazzling show, the Hyannisport Water Cave

00:44.560 --> 00:47.560
of 1962.

00:47.560 --> 00:59.920
The gallery at the Ironomic Golf Club near Philadelphia sees the PGA jinx work against

00:59.920 --> 01:02.520
Arnold Palmer, fresh from his British open win.

01:02.520 --> 01:08.360
He has never won the PGA, and today rivals like Bob Golby run far ahead.

01:08.360 --> 01:12.360
Golby is hot on the fourth and final round, and the crowd sees him duel down to the final

01:12.360 --> 01:19.440
hole with Gary Player and the South African Whiz Kid.

01:19.440 --> 01:24.440
In this 44th professional golfer scramble, the crowd is treated to a brand of Supergolf

01:24.440 --> 01:26.480
along with some heartbreakers.

01:26.480 --> 01:31.000
When George Bayer misses a two-footer on the 18th, he has to settle with a tie for third

01:31.000 --> 01:40.000
place.

01:40.000 --> 01:44.840
There's high drama on the 18th green as Player eases a 35-footer to within two feet of the

01:44.840 --> 01:45.840
cup.

01:45.840 --> 01:50.360
That gives him a short one for a par four and the match, unless Bob Golby could have

01:50.360 --> 01:53.960
come up with a birdie from 25 feet out.

01:53.960 --> 02:01.960
He's close, but he stays one stroke behind.

02:01.960 --> 02:07.000
Then the $13,000 putt that makes Player the first nonresident to win this title.

02:07.000 --> 02:10.120
He appears overwhelmed as he lifts the ball from the cup.

02:10.120 --> 02:14.760
Gary Player, with his card of 278, takes the title by one stroke.

02:14.760 --> 02:20.160
A storybook finish.

02:20.160 --> 02:28.860
The greatest field and track spectacle in the US since the 1932 Olympics as Russian

02:28.860 --> 02:31.320
and American track aces clash.

02:31.320 --> 02:35.480
In the women's 100 meters, it's long-legged Wilma Rudolph Ward who streaks away from the

02:35.480 --> 02:39.480
field to prove once more she is the fastest woman in the world.

02:39.480 --> 02:50.840
Two from each nation compete in every event and it's a Russian who runs second.

02:50.840 --> 02:55.240
This is the fourth time the Soviets have met with the US and in the men's 100 meter dash,

02:55.240 --> 02:58.040
Bob Hayes equals the meet record set in 1958.

02:58.040 --> 03:08.560
When Mr. Hayes gets going, the X-15 couldn't catch him.

03:08.560 --> 03:12.640
Ralph Boston runs up a record all his own as he beats the Russians for the fourth time

03:12.640 --> 03:19.440
with a leap of 26.9.

03:19.440 --> 03:23.880
As the US rolls up a handy point lead, Hal Connolly contributes his bit as he sets a

03:23.880 --> 03:34.240
world mark in the hammer throw with 231 feet 10 inches, a mighty heave as that.

03:34.240 --> 03:40.120
The second day of the two-day meet sees attendance go over 150,000 and a program of thrills begins

03:40.120 --> 03:41.120
with the women's hurdles.

03:41.120 --> 03:45.360
The Russians prove too good for the US duo as they run one-two.

03:45.360 --> 03:50.960
Irina Press and Ilya Kuklova spark a Russian victory in the women's field events, but for

03:50.960 --> 03:59.360
the first time in four years, their margin of victory is narrow.

03:59.360 --> 04:03.600
Feature event of the day is the 1500 meter run, the metric mile and all eyes are on little

04:03.600 --> 04:06.520
Jim Beatty, co-captain of the American team.

04:06.520 --> 04:09.800
He's leading in the last lap with victory closer at every step.

04:09.800 --> 04:21.480
And need be, the mighty might has a reserve he seems to turn on at will.

04:21.480 --> 04:31.600
Beatty is across to set a new American record as well as a new meet mark.

04:31.600 --> 04:33.560
Climax of the meet comes in the high jump.

04:33.560 --> 04:39.920
With everyone else out, Russia's Valerie Brummel goes over 7'1", 7'3", and finally 7 feet 5

04:39.920 --> 04:41.880
inches for a new world's record.

04:41.880 --> 05:04.000
The high-flying Russian takes a new record home, but the US takes the meet on points.

